 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The median texture construction is $O(N \cdot V)$ where $N$ is the number of views and $V$ is the number of texels, but the online estimator allows for $O(1)$ memory per texel update [S2]. The environment map initialization is $O(L)$ where $L$ is the number of lobes [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The online median estimator avoids storing all projected views, keeping memory usage low even for tens of thousands of views [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Prevents optimization from getting stuck in degenerate solutions (all-zero or all-one albedo) [S1]. Suppresses view-dependent artifacts in diffuse color estimation [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption that the median intensity of input images is a good proxy for neutral lighting [S1]. The spherical Fibonacci lattice is an approximation of uniform distribution [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When training inverse rendering models with Spherical Gaussians or similar BRDF representations where environment map and material parameters are optimized jointly [S1, S2].
-*   **When not to use:** If the input images have extreme exposure variations that cannot be normalized by a single global scale factor [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** Initialization is generally fast, dominated by the computation of the median texture or the generation of the spherical Fibonacci lattice. The median texture computation scales linearly with the number of views and texels.
+- **Memory Complexity:** The median texture requires memory proportional to the number of texels. An online median estimator helps manage memory for large numbers of views [S2].
+- **Strengths:** Prevents optimization divergence by starting from neutral, physically plausible values. The median texture initialization effectively suppresses specular highlights, leading to better albedo estimates [S2].
+- **Limitations:** The quality of the initial environment map depends on the accuracy of the amplitude scaling, which may require a preliminary forward pass [S1]. The method assumes that the input images are reasonably well-exposed and aligned.
 
 ## Background
-This initialization strategy was developed for the PhySG pipeline [S1] and the Material and Lighting Reconstruction method [S2]. PhySG was introduced in 2021 [S1], and the material reconstruction method was also published in 2021 [S2]. The methods are used in physics-based material editing and relighting applications [S1].
+This initialization strategy is primarily used in inverse rendering pipelines that reconstruct materials and lighting from images, such as PhySG [S1] and texture-space differentiable rendering methods [S2]. It addresses the challenge of optimizing complex material and lighting parameters, which are sensitive to initial values and can lead to poor local minima if not initialized carefully. The use of median textures for albedo initialization was introduced to handle view-dependent effects in multi-view reconstruction [S2].
 
